{"causes": ["The outbreak of the First Punic War in 264 BCE necessitated the creation of a Roman fleet to challenge Carthaginian naval supremacy.", "Rome's burgeoning ambition to control Sicily and its strategic position led to direct military confrontation with Carthage.", "The Roman Republic's system of annually elected consuls placed military and political leadership in the hands of prominent aristocratic families like the Cornelii Scipiones."], "effects": ["His capture at the Lipari Islands served as a harsh lesson for the nascent Roman navy, highlighting the need for improved naval tactics and command.", "The ignominious defeat contributed to Rome's rapid development of naval innovations, such as the corvus, to counter Carthaginian maritime superiority.", "His subsequent election as censor demonstrated the Roman Republic's political resilience and its capacity to rehabilitate leaders despite military failures."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "gnaeus-cornelius-scipio-asina", "sourceName": "Gnaeus Cornelius Scipio Asina", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "first-punic-war", "targetName": "First Punic War", "context": "Gnaeus Cornelius Scipio Asina served as a Roman consul and general during the First Punic War (264–241 BCE)."}, {"sourceSlug": "gnaeus-cornelius-scipio-asina", "sourceName": "Gnaeus Cornelius Scipio Asina", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "lipari-islands", "targetName": "Lipari Islands", "context": "Gnaeus Cornelius Scipio Asina was captured at the Battle of the Lipari Islands in 260 BCE during his consular term."}, {"sourceSlug": "hannibal-gisco", "sourceName": "Hannibal Gisco", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "gnaeus-cornelius-scipio-asina", "targetName": "Gnaeus Cornelius Scipio Asina", "context": "Carthaginian admiral Hannibal Gisco orchestrated the ambush and capture of Gnaeus Cornelius Scipio Asina and his fleet at the Lipari Islands in 260 BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "gnaeus-cornelius-scipio-asina", "sourceName": "Gnaeus Cornelius Sc